+## Pre-admission window cap
+
+The soft budget below bounds the clock. It does not bound the work, and until CCE-169 nothing did: the review window was a lookback from a baseline that advances slowly, so a stalled baseline widened by a day every night and each run finished a smaller fraction of it. The only truncation was the time-based cut inside the admission loop, which fires after the run has already begun failing to keep up.
+
+`resolve_window_cap` (`scripts/orchestrator_runner.py:resolve_window_cap`) resolves `run.window_pr_cap` in config over `DEFAULT_WINDOW_PR_CAP` — 10 (`scripts/orchestrator_runner.py:DEFAULT_WINDOW_PR_CAP`). `0` is the advertised opt-out and restores the pre-CCE-169 unbounded window. The key is **default-ON**, unlike `citation_source_roots` and `lint.external_repos`: every CCE-169 incident happened on a host that had configured nothing, and an opt-in guard against an unrecoverable failure is discovered by having the failure. A cap set too tight is a visible nightly reason an operator raises in one edit; a cap set too loose is a silent stall.
+
+The cut runs on the oldest-first PR list, before the admission loop. PRs past the cap become `window_capped`, which is a **third category** — not a reuse of `admission_deferred`. The two have the same shape and different causes (`admission_deferred` means the run TRIED and ran out of time; `window_capped` means the run deliberately DID NOT TRY), and per CCE-144 classification follows the call site, never the resemblance. Three consumers, and only the first takes them:
