+- **`window_prs` → `next_deferral_counts`** — NO. `next_deferral_counts` (`scripts/orchestrator_runner.py:next_deferral_counts`) pops the entry of any in-window PR that is not still-deferred, and a capped PR can never be still-deferred. Leaving it in `window_prs` would DELETE its genuine deferral history every night it waits.
+- **`_deferred_all` → `partition_deferrals`** — NO. The CCE-140 deferral-skip hatch (`scripts/orchestrator_runner.py:partition_deferrals`) must never abandon a PR the run never attempted.
+
+A capped run is partial with a `held_back_window_capped: n of m PRs held for a later run (cap c)` reason, `degraded=True` (CCE-144 — the run HELD BACK what it did not process; it did not consume and lose it). It is a plain literal, deliberately not routed through the `time_budget` / `held_back` reason selector, which only discriminates truncated-vs-degraded and would render a cap as `time_budget_window_capped` on a truncated run — factually wrong, since the cap fires before any clock is consulted. Such a run still auto-merges: `held_back` is non-empty, so the advance is cursor-backed, so CCE-140's `partial and not advance_cursor_backed` carve-out permits the merge — which is what promotes `state.json` and lets the next run take the next `c` PRs.
